# gap[108]. The UNTYPED file-scope binding — the half of gap[066] the guard
# could not see.
#
# `g066_written_file_scope_global.id` next to this file spells its bindings
# `total: i64 = 0`, which the parser hands module scope as a DECLARATION, and
# the precheck guard added for gap[066] scanned exactly two statement kinds:
# `.local_decl` and `.global_decl`. This program spells the same binding
# `total = 5`. There is no `local` / `var` keyword in idol, so with the type
# annotation gone there is no declaration left to parse — module scope gets an
# `.assign`, the same node `total = 8` produces inside `main`. The guard saw
# nothing and the program ran natively:
#
#     #backend=direct   ok compile   8 5
#     #backend=c                     8 8
#
# C is right. A file-scope binding is ONE storage location; the 8 written in
# `main` must be what `show` reads. The direct backend gave each function its
# own register-resident copy re-materialized from the initializer, so the write
# landed nowhere and the read folded to 5.
#
# Two things worth keeping. The gap[066] guard was not wrong, it was
# INCOMPLETE, and the incompleteness tracked a SPELLING rather than a
# semantics — the identical program is refused with a type annotation and
# admitted without one. And `promote_module_captured_locals` in the same file
# already treated module-scope `.assign` as a binding site, which is what
# makes this an omission rather than a decision.
#
# The precheck now scans module-scope `.assign` targets too, so this program
# takes the honest DNB001 path (`mod-global-written:total`) and answers 8 8
# through C. Native census is unchanged at 129: no corpus program had this
# shape, which is why nothing caught it — hence this fixture.
#
# Promote both files into the corpus proper the day the native profile gives a
# written file-scope binding real storage. The C column already prints `8 8`.

total = 5

show: i64 = ()
  total

main: i64 = ()
  total = 8
  a: i64 = total
  b: i64 = show()
  print(a)
  print(b)
  0
